            <ArticleTitle>Syphilitic coronary artery ostial stenosis resulting in acute myocardial infarction and death.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>25-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cardiovascular abnormalities are well-known manifestations of tertiary syphilis infections. Most notable in this regard is syphilitic aortitis, which tends to result in aortic root dilatation and its associated complications. A less common manifestation of syphilitic aortitis is coronary artery ostial narrowing related to aortic wall thickening. Herein, we present the case of a 32-year-old female who died of a myocardial infarct due to coronary artery ostial stenosis secondary to syphilitic aortitis.</AbstractText>
